\section*{Structural Problems for \$C\textasciicircum{}*\$- and von Neumann Algebras}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
The manuscript claims that the factors L(F\_2) and L(F\_3) are isomorphic, consequently identifying all interpolated free group factors and giving each the full positive-real fundamental group. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 287, 2026-09-23): Small trace-preserving automorphisms are built using finitely supported cocycles on free words. A Catalan-moment computation and truncated inverses provide coefficients that absorb an additional Haar generator with controlled error. Iterating with carefully allocated errors preserves generation as well as the free distribution; established amplification formulas transfer the consecutive-rank construction to ranks two and three.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 287, 2026-09-23): The free-group-factor nonisomorphism clause is refuted by L(F2)\(\cong\)L(F3), but the twelve other operator-algebra questions in this bundle are not thereby answered.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 287, the scope page documents the following selections. The selected normal trace-preserving isomorphisms cover all interpolated free-factor parameters greater than one, including infinity. The fundamental-group consequence is separate. The documented statement covers the main claim of manuscript 1. Comparator files: InterpolatedFactors.lean. Declarations: OAI.FreeGroupFactorMain.Interpolation.allInterpolatedIsomorphic.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

